WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.200
Now let’s tie the boat up.

2
00:00:02.450 --> 00:00:04.275
Look at the diagram.

3
00:00:04.525 --> 00:00:09.800
A boat alongside is usually held by four lines or more, and each has a job.

4
00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:15.200
The bow line leads forward from the bow, and the stern line leads aft from the stern.

5
00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:17.925
Those two hold her in to the dock.

6
00:00:18.175 --> 00:00:21.175
The spring lines stop her moving along it.

7
00:00:21.425 --> 00:00:27.275
The bow spring runs from near the bow, aft to the dock, and it stops the boat surging forward.

8
00:00:27.525 --> 00:00:33.400
The stern spring runs from near the stern, forward to the dock, and it stops her moving back.

9
00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:37.325
A breast line runs straight across, to hold her close.

10
00:00:38.325 --> 00:00:41.425
One more thing, and it matters in Florida.

11
00:00:41.675 --> 00:00:43.700
Leave slack for the tide.

12
00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:47.775
What happens if you tie her up tight at high water and go home?

13
00:00:48.025 --> 00:00:52.175
The tide falls, and the boat ends up hanging from her own lines.
